WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
A muscle channel opens almost at once; a heart receptor waits on a G protein.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.120
Now watch estradiol, a fat-soluble hormone, slip through the membrane with no relay.

3
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.140
Why can that route still take hours?

4
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.810
Each step from binding to output must finish before the next starts.

5
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.530
Onset time is roughly the sum of those waits.

6
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.650
A muscle channel needs only binding and opening.

7
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:35.870
Each takes tens of microseconds, finishing well under a millisecond.

8
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:43.213
The heart route has four steps, and exchange, swapping GDP for GTP, is the slowest.

9
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:49.253
Alone, a G protein releases GDP 0.44 times a minute.

10
00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:54.953
1 ÷ 0.44 is a mean wait of about 2.3 minutes.

11
00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:00.167
Suppose the occupied receptor speeds release a thousandfold.

12
00:01:09.127 --> 00:01:13.827
1 ÷ 7.3 gives a mean wait near 140 ms.

13
00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:17.047
But a cell holds many G proteins.

14
00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:20.027
Must it wait that long for current?

15
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.700
No. The fraction on by time t is 1 minus e to the minus 7 .3 t.

16
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:38.460
That reaches 10% percent at about 14 ms, and current can start.

17
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.540
Channels need several G protein pieces, a little extra delay.

18
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.600
So steps set when: waits add up, and the slowest sets the pace.

19
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.160
Now slower designs.

20
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.720
Single-pass kinase receptors cross the membrane once and carry a phosphate-attaching

21
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:04.480
enzyme. A ligand pairs chains, or rearranges a pair, so the enzymes tag each other

22
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.520
and recruit partners.

23
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.660
Those steps take seconds to minutes.

24
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:16.183
Now the puzzle. Estradiol slips inside and binds its nuclear receptor, skipping

25
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:23.203
every relay. But its output is new RNA within minutes, then new protein over tens of

26
00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:27.723
minutes to hours. One slow step outweighs every skipped relay.

27
00:02:28.817 --> 00:02:34.477
Duration follows the same rule: signals last until their slowest off step finishes.

28
00:02:36.137 --> 00:02:40.217
Muscle current decays in about a millisecond once acetylcholine clears.

29
00:02:41.697 --> 00:02:45.197
G proteins stay on for seconds until they split GTP.

30
00:02:45.197 --> 00:02:50.717
New proteins can keep a gene program running after the hormone leaves.

31
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.260
This rule has limits.

32
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.700
High enzymatic gain can buy back speed, because multiplied output crosses a

33
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:05.440
threshold sooner. Potency, how much ligand a route needs, is separate from speed.

34
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.760
And estradiol has reported rapid surface actions.

35
00:03:10.833 --> 00:03:12.873
Design does more than count steps.

36
00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:18.473
The slowest waits, on and off, set when a signal starts and how long it lasts.
